When designing a robust offline translation app for mobile, I quickly realized that feeding raw user text straight into heavy neural models causes subword tokenizers to break down, drop words, or generate garbage. To solve this, I architected a 4-layer local pipeline optimized specifically for Android, balancing performance, memory limits, and complex low-resource grammar.

Here is how my system breaks down from input to UI rendering:
Layer 1: Preprocessing & Normalization (Defensive Boundary)

Translation models are extremely sensitive to formatting. This layer guarantees that only standardized, grammatically sound text gets through to my models.

    User Input Interface: Supports natural typing in English or Hindi, accommodating spelling errors, phonetic Romanized Hindi (Hinglish like “khana thanda hai”), or native scripts.

    English SymSpell Typo Engine: Uses a local SQLite frequency database (en_typos.db) via a slang filter to measure Levenshtein edit distance (up to 2 character insertions, deletions, or swaps) against common English vocabulary in under 1 millisecond without neural compute overhead.

    Unicode Script Detection: A 5-line ASCII filter inspects raw character bytes when Hindi is selected. If it finds ASCII (a-z), I know it's Hinglish. If it detects Devanagari characters directly from a Hindi keyboard, I skip transliteration entirely to save CPU cycles.

    Hinglish Transliteration: Checks an indexed SQLite table for instant O(1) matches (e.g., mapping “khana” to “खाना”). If a proper noun or rare word is missing, it routes to a compact 4MB IndicXlit ONNX model for phonetic conversion.

    Santali Character Normalizer: Because Ol Chiki is a phonemic alphabet rather than a complex abugida, Romanized Santali maps cleanly via a deterministic 1:1 character lookup table directly into Unicode Ol Chiki characters without needing a heavy neural model.

Layer 2: The English Bridge (MarianMT via ONNX)

Instead of packaging multiple 800MB IndicTrans2 models for every direction, I introduced an ultra-lightweight translation bridge running directly in Kotlin using Microsoft's onnxruntime-android.

    Model Architecture: Quantized INT8 MarianMT models (opus-mt-en-hi and opus-mt-hi-en), which are 6-layer encoder-decoder transformers optimized for European-to-Indic pairs.

    Storage & Memory: Each quantized model takes up only ~40MB on disk and consumes ~35MB of RAM during inference, running safely alongside my main app without triggering Android's low-memory killer.

    Execution Speed: Blazingly fast, taking roughly 30ms to 50ms on standard ARM mobile CPUs.

    The Pivot Mechanism:

        English input → Layer 2 translates to intermediate clean Hindi → populates secondary UI → feeds Layer 3 for Santali.

        Hindi input → Layer 2 translates to English → populates secondary UI.

        Santali input → Layer 3 translates to Hindi first → Layer 2 converts to English.

Layer 3: The Core Indic Translation Engine (IndicTrans2 via C++)

Translating between Hindi and Santali is uniquely difficult because Santali is a low-resource language with complex agglutinative grammar. Generic models fail here.

    Model Core: AI4Bharat's distilled 200M parameter IndicTrans2 model, quantized to INT8 (~250MB) and paired with SentencePiece subword tokenizers (model.SRC and model.TGT).

    C++ NDK Execution: Runs inside the native C++ runtime via CTranslate2. It utilizes custom INT8 matrix multiplication routines optimized for ARM NEON instructions, completely bypassing Android JVM overhead.

    Linguistic Syntax Alignment: Because both Hindi and Santali follow a Subject-Object-Verb (SOV) structure, translating strictly between them inside IndicTrans2 prevents word order hallucinations and preserves Ol Chiki suffix accuracy.

    Inference Configuration: Runs across 4 dedicated CPU performance cores with beam_size = 3, a max generation length of 256 tokens, and a repetition penalty of 1.5 could be (1.3 or 1.2 )to eliminate infinite punctuation loops.

Layer 4: Lifecycle, Memory & UI Synchronization

Running a 250MB C++ translation engine alongside an ONNX model on a mobile device requires strict operating system coordination to avoid app termination.

    Hardware Containment via JNI: Kotlin communicates with C++ through JNI. The heavy IndicTrans2 model is wrapped in a native std::unique_ptr<ctranslate2::Translator>, keeping model memory safely isolated in C++ instead of polluting the Android Garbage Collector heap.

    Lifecycle Awareness: When the app is minimized (onStop()), my JNI bridge calls unloadNativeTranslator(), executing .reset() on the smart pointer to completely purge the 250MB model from RAM. When the app returns (onResume()), I rebuild the engine asynchronously on a background worker thread.

    Sequential Execution: Layer 2 (ONNX) and Layer 3 (CTranslate2) execute sequentially rather than in parallel. This prevents CPU core starvation and keeps peak transient memory under 350MB.

    UI Thread Sync: Once both target strings are generated, my worker thread posts results back to the Android Main/UI thread using runOnUiThread. Santali text renders cleanly in an Ol Chiki-compatible typeface (Noto Sans Ol Chiki), while secondary translations display in standard system fonts.




